    "q-179401": {
      "dom_id": "step-q179401",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "$(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}=${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{6}{10}-\\frac{5}{10})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that ${2}^{-4}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{16}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{1+16}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{17}{16}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
